## Windows native playback prototype — 2026-10-05

The separate `native-player-prototype.ts` entry now implements mpv process control through Windows named-pipe JSON IPC. Its local Vue page uses only `window.nativePlayerLab`; Server business APIs remain HTTP. Main owns an origin-scoped authentication session and a streaming loopback proxy for one selected raw media file. The prototype uses separate build output/profile and never loads from production main/preload.

The prototype now uses an independently written Win32 helper HWND + mpv `wid` underneath a transparent owned Electron UI window. The helper exclusively synchronizes physical client bounds through SetWindowPos and checks the overlay belongs to its parent PID; main synchronizes visibility and exposes only fullscreen/minimize/close enums to the trusted local frame. The overlay cannot move or resize independently. Native ownership alone does not bind geometry; main must not reapply rounded DIP bounds. The 2026-10-06 regression test launches the actual Electron entry and verifies edge hit tests, exact physical geometry, minimize/restore and orderly close. Default mpv OSC/input is disabled. Helper exits on parent death or stdin EOF; main saves/stops mpv before disposing the UI/host. Shared `PlayerTransportControls`, `MoviePartSelect` and `PlayerPlaybackSettingsMenu` preserve Curated controls; the latter hides Web mode selection for native playback. Native-specific transparency and background throttling settings stay in the separate entry.

This is a usable development prototype with native video/Web UI composition verified on Windows. Production routing/engine integration, capture/PiP, track selection/HDR, engine distribution and macOS remain pending. Real problem-file and mixed-DPI monitor comparisons are also pending; synthetic playback checks do not establish that all stuttering is fixed. This qualifies the general future-mpv statements below. See `docs/plan/2026-10-05-windows-native-player-prototype.md` §12 (REQ-0058).

## Release batches — 2026-10-01 (current)

This supersedes older release policies below. New source supports one **Curated YYYYMMDD** Release, with **-2**, **-3**, etc. for later Beijing-date batches, containing Server, Desktop or both. `pnpm release:prepare` inspects each module against its published source; `--write` prepares independent versions, immutable batch metadata and Notes for review. No change means no release. `release:version:show` shows both source versions. CD consumes the batch and never creates Full; old all-in-one CLI/publication triggers are disabled. Shared/unknown delivery inputs conservatively affect both modules and need scope review. Same-source retries reuse versions and batch identity.

About & Updates remains per-component: Desktop uses desktop.json; new Server uses server-v2.json. The first Server-containing batch uses a legacy-compatible server-v tag but a dated title, and publishes both Server feeds; old server.json remains pinned to this bridge thereafter. New date-tagged Server releases require the bridge. Both components can share the bridge Release. Latest selects the greatest dated batch, with historical Full fallback until the first batch. No new HTTP API or library-config setting. Implementation is local until explicitly pushed/published; real Windows/Mac package gates remain in CD. Current operations: docs/guide.md §8; design/history: docs/plan/2026-03-31-production-packaging-and-config-strategy.md.

# Architecture Boundaries

## Documentation Truth

- `docs/product/2026-03-20-jav-libary.md` describes both current product behavior and target architecture. Current code is the source of truth when documents conflict with implementation.
- In documents, clearly label current, target, and pending items.

## Implemented Current State

- Go HTTP backend, SQLite application database, scanning, scraping, task system, and REST API under `backend/` are implemented.
- The frontend can use real HTTP through `VITE_USE_WEB_API=true`.

## Desktop Shell Boundaries

- Electron now has an MVP shell under `electron/`: for the default local legacy target it starts or reuses the Go HTTP backend, waits for `/api/health`, starts or reuses Vite at `http://127.0.0.1:5173` in development, uses the Curated app icon and tray, loads the existing Web UI in BrowserWindow, and hides to tray when the window is closed. Packaged runtime still loads the backend-hosted static UI on `http://127.0.0.1:8081`.
- Update actions distinguish local legacy installations from remote/unknown targets. `getDesktopInfo()` includes main-owned `serverOrigin`; HTTP app-update responses carry request-local `localUpdateAllowed`, and remote download/install/cache-delete calls are rejected. Standalone Desktop uses its own update channel; current Desktop delivery remains manual-download-only.
- Electron preload/main process IPC remains deliberately narrow: current code exposes `window.javLibrary.pickDirectory()` for native directory selection, a read-only `windowChrome` capability for platform-specific window styling, plus `getDesktopInfo()` / `checkDesktopUpdate()` for local component metadata and manifest checking (current development / legacy builds do not enter standalone update feeds). Business APIs remain on HTTP. Deep business IPC, mpv process control, and broader desktop file/permission bridges remain future desktop concerns unless current code proves otherwise.
- Vue renderer code must not assume Electron, mpv, PotPlayer, or any native-player executable exists.

- Desktop connection management is local and independent of Server UI: `servers.json` in userData, a bundled connection window, origin-scoped persistent sessions, probe/load-before-switch and offline recovery. The current trusted Server UI can read a connection summary and request a saved-ID switch with native confirmation; the current trusted main-window frame can add a new record through addServer, without overwriting IDs or duplicate origins. Editing/deleting remain restricted to the exact local manager main frame. Settings → Network & devices displays the saved list and current connection status, with an in-page Add server dialog. Saved remote targets never trigger local backend fallback. Apple Silicon standalone Desktop DMG/ZIP packaging is implemented; Windows component installers are also implemented; SSDP remains pending.

## Storage Directory Ownership

- Storage roots belong to Server. HTTP health exposes request-local `canManageLibraryPaths`; remote/unknown clients render read-only movie/comic/photo directory information. Three-library root CRUD, movie reveal/rebind and default import target changes are enforced as direct Server-local operations. Uploads still use Server-configured destinations. Mock remains editable; local standalone Desktop can manage directories independently of its update distribution.

## Server Administration Ownership

- Server-wide settings writes and administrative APIs require direct loopback access. Remote clients retain device preferences, current-session lock/unlock, Desktop connections and ordinary library usage. Settings responses redact AI and proxy credentials remotely; hidden navigation and deep links share the local-access policy. See guide: Remote settings access.

## Frontend Business Code

- HLS session identity belongs to a playback instance, not a movie: creating a new session must not evict another client's session for the same movie. The renderer releases its previous session after new media data is available and cleans up cancelled/late requests. Startup capacity is bounded and cancellable.

- Rendering code depends on composables, frontend service contracts, and shared types.
- State management currently defaults to composables plus the service layer. Pinia or a similar store may be introduced incrementally for a small, bounded service or new feature when it clearly improves maintainability; avoid a big-bang migration or broad parallel global store.
- Views/components must not import concrete Web/Mock adapters directly for library behavior. Use `useLibraryService()` and add service-contract methods when a new capability is needed.
- Native-player handoff must stay optional and disabled by default when not configured. Express it through service settings and browser protocol templates, not hard-coded runtime assumptions.
- When adding a new capability, define DTOs/error codes/event names first, then wire the current HTTP adapter or a future stdio/IPC adapter behind the same service contract.


## Split release implementation (2026-09-27)

Windows Full/Server/Desktop installers and ZIPs are now implemented in `component_cd.py` / `windows_components.py`; Apple Silicon Desktop DMG/ZIP shares the Desktop version. Full embeds the exact standalone installers, uses their component identities, preserves newer installations and reports partial failures. Server runs independently in the current-user tray; Desktop only connects. Component releases use `full-v` / `server-v` / `desktop-v` tags and isolated stable manifests on `release-channels`; GitHub Latest follows the newest stable Full; old all-in-one users migrate manually once. Server rejects legacy installer cache; local Desktop connections expose independent Desktop update checks. Initial versions: Server 1.6.0, Desktop 0.1.0, Full 1.6.0. Old-install detection requires the documented backup/manual migration; automated custom/account migration remains pending; SSDP is implemented in 1.7.0. Windows real installer/lifecycle smoke checks gate CD. See guide §8 for exact names, update isolation and recovery.


## 2026-09-27 worktree integration

Desktop 0.2.0 uses the bundled Vue launcher and starts independently (`pnpm dev:electron` no longer starts Go/Vite). Server 1.7.0 adds public `/api/server-info`, persistent `<databasePath>.server-id`, optional IPv4 SSDP and `/discovery/description.xml`. `discoveryEnabled` requires LAN listening; changes apply after restart. Released `servers.json` and origin sessions are preserved; development `connections.json` imports atomically only when no released profile exists. Canonical CD/names/version/update isolation remains authoritative; package.yml delegates to it in draft mode. Upgrade details and limitations: docs/guide.md, “Desktop discovery and upgrade”. These facts supersede earlier development/pending notes above.


## Full 1.7.2 legacy upgrade implementation (2026-09-27)

Source now targets Full/Server 1.7.2 (Desktop 0.2.0 unchanged). Full embeds `cmd/curated-migrate`: same-user legacy detection, verified offline backup under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Curated\installer-migrations`, internal old uninstall (UAC only for machine-wide removal), independent component install and retry journal. Standalone Server reads `server-startup.json` to preserve the original external data root/custom config on normal and login startup. No new HTTP API or library-config keys. Registered 1.x all-in-one versions below 1.6 are recognized; real 1.4.13/1.5.8 CD baselines added. Running processes, ambiguous installations, relative/program-local data and linked program trees are blocked before removal. Users must quit from tray; no forced termination or cross-account migration. Saved connections copy only if the new profile is empty; authentication sessions are not merged. Local tests/cross-compilation passed; Windows Inno/UAC/install gates pending. Published 1.7.1 remains unchanged; see guide “Migrating an old all-in-one installation”. This supersedes earlier manual-only statements for the new source, not historical packages.

## Windows installer localization and shutdown — 2026-09-27

Source targets Full/Server **1.7.3**, Desktop **0.2.1**. Windows setup has Simplified Chinese and English language selection, localized migration/upgrade prompts, and Full passes its selected language to component installers. A repository-vendored Inno translation avoids build-host language dependencies. The embedded `curated-migrate -action stop` helper uses exact installation executable paths, PID creation times, same-user SID/session checks and non-forced Restart Manager shutdown; it waits for exit before component file replacement. Full uses the same shutdown step before the existing verified backup and legacy uninstall. Split components retain their AppId and upgrade in place; library data is preserved. Refused/uninspectable shutdown stops the upgrade. Desktop launcher now handles session shutdown; Server ignores cancelled shutdown. No new HTTP API or library-config fields. Local Inno compile checks, real Windows process fixtures, Go tests/vet and Electron tests/build passed; real historical-package/UAC acceptance remains in CD. Published packages are immutable; changed component installers require new versions rather than reusing 1.7.2/0.2.0. See guide “Migrating an old all-in-one installation”. This supersedes the earlier manual-exit requirement for the new source only.

## Latest policy — 2026-09-27 (supersedes legacy pinning)

GitHub Latest now selects the highest public stable Full SemVer; standalone Server/Desktop keep independent component manifests. Component preflight no longer depends on a legacy Latest. Component, legacy and recovery publication share `release_lib/latest_release.py`: list all pages, ignore drafts/prereleases and single-component tags, validate the matching nonempty installer, then set/verify Latest without changing public assets or visibility. No Full means highest legacy fallback. Manual `Release - Reconcile Latest` shares the publication lock. Old installed all-in-one binaries cannot be remotely patched: verified v1.5.8 rejects Full tags and requires a one-time manual Full migration. Full 1.7.3 has passed Windows/Mac CD and is published. No new runtime/API/config changes. See guide §8 and docs/plan/2026-09-27-latest-release-channel-separation.md.

## 2026-10-02 playback ownership

The application shell now owns a single scoped player host. Native video Picture-in-Picture may retain the same `PlayerPage`, video and HLS instance across business-page navigation; renderer replacement/server switches and auth lock terminate playback. A frozen-origin route projection keeps playback/playlist context separate from the page being browsed. The player remains an HTTP/service-layer browser implementation, with no new business IPC or native-player assumptions. Background playback requires an active native PiP window; normal page exits still release media. `SidebarPlaybackEntry` supplies the single status/control surface through host-registered media operations; `ActivePlaybackHost` renders only the playback surface, with no separate floating dock. Details: docs/plan/2026-10-01-picture-in-picture-audit-and-optimization.md.
